    <div class="crs-pin-detail is-open" data-detail="m1-roles-0"><strong>The Consultant</strong><p>You explain market conditions, pricing, and property value in plain language — not just repeat what's printed on the listing sheet — so the client can actually make an informed decision.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m1-roles-1"><strong>The Project Manager</strong><p>You keep a multi-week (sometimes multi-month) transaction moving — following up with lenders, developers, and title officers so nothing quietly stalls while the client waits.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m1-roles-2"><strong>The Negotiator</strong><p>You protect the client's interests at the table — on price, payment terms, and timelines — even when it would be easier to just go along with what the other side wants.</p></div>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">1.1.3 The Complete Sales Cycle</span>
    <p>Every deal, no matter the property type, moves through the same six stages. Knowing exactly where a client sits in this cycle at any moment is what lets you anticipate their next question before they ask it, instead of always reacting a step behind.</p>
    <div class="crs-cycle">
        <div class="crs-cycle-step"><span>1</span><strong>Prospecting</strong><small>Generating leads through referrals, social media, broker networks, and site visit sign-ups.</small></div>
        <div class="crs-cycle-step"><span>2</span><strong>Discovery</strong><small>Understanding the buyer's budget, must-haves, financing plan, and timeline.</small></div>
        <div class="crs-cycle-step"><span>3</span><strong>Showing</strong><small>Guiding site visits or virtual tours, highlighting both the unit and the community.</small></div>
        <div class="crs-cycle-step"><span>4</span><strong>Negotiating</strong><small>Handling price, payment terms, reservation fees, and move-in timelines.</small></div>
        <div class="crs-cycle-step"><span>5</span><strong>Escrow / Closing</strong><small>Reservation, Contract to Sell, loan processing, Deed of Absolute Sale, and title transfer.</small></div>
        <div class="crs-cycle-step"><span>6</span><strong>Post-Sale</strong><small>Turnover support, warranty concerns, and building the relationship for referrals.</small></div>
    </div>
    <p>Notice that "closing the sale" is only stage 4 of 6 — negotiating is the midpoint, not the finish line. Modules 3 through 6 of this course each zoom into one or more of these later stages in detail.</p>
    <div class="crs-scenario">
        <strong>🇵🇭 Philippine Scenario</strong>
        <p>A buyer messages you on Facebook about a 2-bedroom condo unit in Pasig. Instead of only sending the price list, you ask about their financing plan, ideal move-in date, and must-haves. This lets you shortlist 2–3 truly matching units instead of flooding them with 15 random listings — saving both of you time and building early trust.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-takeaway-line"><strong>Core Takeaway:</strong> You are not just selling a physical structure — you are managing a complex, multi-step transaction where your primary value is keeping the deal on track and minimizing client stress.</div>
</article>

<article class="crs-lesson">
    <h3><span class="crs-lesson-num">Lesson 1.2</span> Navigating Buyer Expectations</h3>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">1.2.1 Buyers Already Did Their Homework</span>
    <p>Filipino buyers today rarely walk in blind. Before they ever message you, they've usually scrolled listing sites and developer pages, compared photos, and even estimated their own monthly amortization with an online calculator. That changes what they actually need from you — it isn't more information, they already have plenty of that. What they need is your judgment, honesty, and speed.</p>
    <p>This is a genuine shift from how the industry worked a decade ago, when the agent was often the buyer's only source of listings and pricing. Now the information gap has closed, but a trust and interpretation gap has opened up in its place — and that gap is exactly where you add value.</p>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">1.2.2 The Old Way vs. What Buyers Expect Now</span>
    <p>Tap each card below to see the shift.</p>
    <div class="crs-compare-grid">
        <div class="crs-compare-card">
            <div class="crs-compare-head"><strong>😐 The Old Way</strong><svg class="crs-compare-toggle" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"/></svg></div>
            <div class="crs-compare-body"><p>Send the full price list and wait for the buyer to ask questions. Highlight only the best features and stay quiet about anything unflattering. Reply whenever there's time.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="crs-compare-card">
            <div class="crs-compare-head"><strong>✅ What Buyers Expect Now</strong><svg class="crs-compare-toggle" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"/></svg></div>
            <div class="crs-compare-body"><p>Ask about budget, must-haves, and timeline first, then shortlist 2–3 units that actually fit. Volunteer the flaws — noise, dues, flood history — before they have to ask. Reply within a few hours, not days.</p></div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Also worth remembering:</strong> local insight is something no app can give them — traffic patterns, nearby developments, association dues trends, and upcoming infrastructure. And at every stage, give clear next steps so they're never left guessing what happens after this. A simple "here's what happens next, and here's roughly when" at the end of every conversation does more for client confidence than most agents realize.</p>
    <div class="crs-scenario">
        <strong>🇵🇭 Philippine Scenario</strong>
        <p>A client asks about flood risk in a subdivision in Cavite. Instead of avoiding the question, you share what you actually know — drainage history, elevation, and whether the area flooded in past typhoons — and offer to confirm specifics with the developer. This honesty is what turns a one-time inquiry into a signed reservation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-takeaway-line"><strong>Core Takeaway:</strong> Because buyers already have the data, they expect you to provide the interpretation. They expect speed in communication, absolute transparency regarding property flaws, and local market expertise that an app cannot provide.</div>
</article>

<article class="crs-lesson">
    <h3><span class="crs-lesson-num">Lesson 1.3</span> Professional Conduct &amp; Daily Operations</h3>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">1.3.1 Consistency Over Talent</span>
    <p>Top-producing agents usually aren't the most naturally talented people in the room — they're the most <strong>consistent</strong>. Three daily habits build a professional, trustworthy brand, often before a client ever meets you in person.</p>
    <p>None of these habits are complicated on their own. What separates agents who compound their reputation over years from agents who plateau is simply doing them every single day, including the days when there's no active deal to justify the effort.</p>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">1.3.2 Three Daily Habits</span>
    <p>Tap each habit to see what it looks like in practice.</p>
