            <span class="crs-pin-label">Standardized Communication</span>
            <svg class="crs-pin-check" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail is-open" data-detail="m1-habits-0"><strong>Time Blocking</strong><p>Reserving fixed hours for prospecting, follow-ups, and site visits instead of reacting to whatever comes in — so urgent-but-unimportant messages don't quietly crowd out the activities that actually generate deals.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m1-habits-1"><strong>CRM Discipline</strong><p>Logging every lead, conversation, and follow-up date in one place, rather than in your head or scattered across chat threads — so nobody falls through the cracks when you're juggling a dozen active clients.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m1-habits-2"><strong>Standardized Communication</strong><p>Using consistent templates for common messages — inquiry replies, showing confirmations, post-visit follow-ups — so quality doesn't depend on how tired you are that day.</p></div>

    <p>Taken together, these three habits are really about one thing: making your professionalism visible before a client ever verifies it themselves. A prompt, organized, consistent agent signals competence long before the first site visit happens.</p>
    <div class="crs-scenario">
        <strong>🇵🇭 Philippine Scenario</strong>
        <p>You block 8–9 AM every weekday for CRM follow-ups before the day's calls start. A lead who inquired two weeks ago about a Cavite house-and-lot gets a scheduled check-in message exactly when you promised, instead of being forgotten — and replies that afternoon ready to schedule a visit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-takeaway-line"><strong>Core Takeaway:</strong> Consistency, not raw talent, is what separates top-producing agents from amateurs. Time blocking, CRM discipline, and standardized communication build a professional brand before a client ever meets you.</div>
</article>

<div class="crs-callout">
    <strong>📌 TAKE NOTE — Adopt the Consultant Mindset</strong>
    <p>Throughout this entire course, one idea repeats in different forms: act as a trusted advisor who protects the client's financial interests, not as someone reading a brochure aloud. If you internalize that mindset now, most of the specific techniques in later modules will feel like common sense rather than scripts to memorize.</p>
</div>

<div class="crs-key-takeaways">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Your primary value is interpreting market data and managing the full transaction — not simply unlocking units.</li>
        <li>Every deal moves through the same six stages: Prospecting, Discovery, Showing, Negotiating, Escrow/Closing, and Post-Sale.</li>
        <li>Buyers already have the data; they need your transparency, speed, and local expertise on top of it.</li>
        <li>Time blocking, CRM discipline, and standardized communication are what separate consistent professionals from amateurs.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
